<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saul</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as King Saul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saul was, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, the first king of Israel, a man of the tribe of Benjamin anointed by the prophet Samuel who ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibeah">Gibeah</a>, traditionally in the late eleventh century BCE. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a>, Ammonites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalekites">Amalekites</a>, quarrelled with Samuel and with his commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, and died with his sons at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mount-gilboa">Battle of Mount Gilboa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saul.jpg" alt="Saul" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernst Josephson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saul_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, Saul was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, of the family of the Matrites, and came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibeah">Gibeah</a>. He married Ahinoam, daughter of Ahimaaz, and had at least five sons, among them Jonathan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ish-bosheth">Ish-bosheth</a>, and two daughters, Merab and Michal. The Books of Samuel give three accounts of his rise. In one he is sent to look for his father's strayed donkeys and is anointed in private by the seer Samuel at Ramah. In another Samuel assembles the people at Mizpah and uses the Urim and Thummim to choose the tribe of Benjamin, the clan of Matri and then Saul. In a third he leads an army to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabesh-gilead">Jabesh-Gilead</a> from the Ammonite king Nahash and is crowned at Gilgal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After relieving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabesh-gilead">Jabesh-Gilead</a>, Saul is said to have fought the Moabites, Ammonites, Edomites, the kings of Zobah, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philistines">Philistines</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalekites">Amalekites</a>. With Jonathan he defeated a large Philistine force at the pass of Michmash, though his rash oath that the army should fast nearly cost Jonathan his life. Samuel then rebuked him for offering sacrifice himself, and later told him that God had rejected him as king after he spared the Amalekite king Agag and the best livestock in a war meant to destroy Amalek completely.</p><p>From then on the narrative turns largely on his troubled relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, the son of Jesse whom Samuel anointed. Saul, troubled by an evil spirit, took David to play the harp for him, and David became his armour bearer. Scholars note that the text portrays Saul positively before David appears and negatively afterwards, and suggest that the David story came from a source loyal to David's house. The reign length in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> text, two years, is regarded as faulty; a reign of 20 or 22 years is thought more probable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Before the Philistine battle at Mount Gilboa, Saul consulted a spirit medium at Endor, and the spirit that appeared as Samuel told him he would lose the battle and his life. First Samuel says he asked his armour bearer to kill him and, when refused, fell on his own sword; Jonathan, Abinadab and Malchishua died with him. A conflicting account in 2 Samuel has an Amalekite claiming to have killed him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ish-bosheth">Ish-bosheth</a> succeeded him for two years before being murdered. The historicity of Saul's kingdom is debated, since the evidence is mostly biblical. In the Qur'an Talut is traditionally identified with Saul, a divinely appointed king chosen by a prophet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The prophet Samuel anointed <b>Saul</b> of the tribe of Benjamin, who ruled from Gibeah; the traditional chronology places his accession in the late eleventh century BCE, around 1047 or 1050 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first letters of its fifteen stanzas spell &quot;Willem van Nassov&quot;, and the stanzas mirror one another around the eighth, which compares William to David fleeing <b>Saul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bible portrays King Nahash as an enemy of <b>Saul</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ammon">Ammon</a></li><li><blockquote>The Philistines adopted the local Semitic language and gods such as Dagon, and became Israel's main rivals in the stories of Samson, <b>Saul</b> and David.</blockquote><a href="/state/philistine-pentapolis">Philistine Pentapolis</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibeah">Gibeah</a>. Died in Mount Gilboa. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.43,35.41" aria-label="Mount Gilboa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5460 984 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Saul"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5910 1254h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5910 1254h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.43,35.41">What was here? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
